Spec: Boundary Design Specification

Purpose:
  Own the focused design documents that define Boundary independently of the current Spec Kit change-system adapter, SpecDD compatibility provider, and Codex agent adapter.

Structure:
  ./spec.md: Product purpose, canonical state, invariants, adapter independence, and migration principles
  ./spec-architecture.md: Provider-neutral core, native contract engine, adapter boundaries, and transient projections
  ./spec-contracts.md: Native contract syntax, ownership, scoped applicability, dependencies, and effective context
  ./spec-agent-instructions.md: Skills, progressive disclosure, agent context composition, and portability
  ./spec-authorization.md: Explicit writes, operation records, Git baselines, authorization epochs, and verification
  ./spec-lifecycle.md: Change-system lifecycle integration, scope expansion, contract evolution, and convergence
  ./spec-v0.1.md: Historical acceptance material for the implemented Spec Kit and SpecDD bridge baseline

Owns:
  ./spec.md
  ./spec-architecture.md
  ./spec-contracts.md
  ./spec-agent-instructions.md
  ./spec-authorization.md
  ./spec-lifecycle.md
  ./spec-v0.1.md

References:
  ../speckit-specdd.sdd
  ./change-boundary.sdd

Must:
  Boundary product concepts remain independent of Spec Kit, SpecDD, and Codex.
  The target persistent-contract model uses native Boundary contracts rather than `.sdd` or SpecDD bootstrap semantics.
  Native contracts use explicit path scope and additive applicability so a more specific owner cannot silently escape broader matching constraints.
  Boundary v1 has no global catch-all contract, implicit file-location inheritance, override semantics, or task-level synthetic operation authority.
  Implementation authorization uses explicit declared writes rather than path mentions in prose.
  Authorization derives fresh scope from canonical change input and the native contract graph rather than a persisted feature Change Boundary.
  One operation is represented by one atomic historical record.
  Implementation and persistent-contract evolution remain distinct operation kinds.
  Agent procedure is progressively disclosed through small stable skills while project-specific effective context is derived on demand.
  Deterministic authorization and verification remain independent of agent prompt compliance.
  Current Spec Kit and SpecDD behavior is documented as transitional where operational documentation still depends on it.
  Focused documents avoid duplicating detailed migration checklists from ./TODO.md.

Done when:
  A maintainer can understand Boundary's product model, native contract semantics, agent instruction architecture, authorization evidence, and lifecycle without treating the current Spec Kit or SpecDD implementation as the architecture.
